.TH THREAD 2
.SH NAME
threadcreate,
proccreate,
threadexits,
threadexitsall,
yield,
threadid,
chancreate,
chanfree,
send,
recv,
nbsend,
nbrecv,
sendp,
recvp,
sendul,
recvul,
alt,
qlock,
qunlock,
canqlock,
rsleep,
rwakeup,
rwakeupall \- thread and proc management
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libthread"
.PP
.nf
.B "Thread_Fn :: #type proc(arg: rawptr)"
.B "Chan_Op :: enum { Send, Recv, Noblock }"
.B "Alt :: struct { c: ^Chan, v: rawptr, op: Chan_Op }"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "threadcreate :: proc(fn: Thread_Fn, arg: rawptr, stacksize := STACK_DEFAULT) -> int"
.B "proccreate :: proc(fn: Thread_Fn, arg: rawptr, stacksize := STACK_DEFAULT) -> i64"
.B "yield :: proc()"
.B "threadid :: proc() -> int"
.B "threadexits :: proc(status: string) -> !"
.B "threadexitsall :: proc(status: string) -> !"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "chancreate :: proc(elemsize: int, nbuf: int) -> ^Chan"
.B "chanfree :: proc(c: ^Chan)"
.B "send :: proc(c: ^Chan, v: rawptr)"
.B "recv :: proc(c: ^Chan, v: rawptr)"
.B "nbsend :: proc(c: ^Chan, v: rawptr) -> bool"
.B "nbrecv :: proc(c: ^Chan, v: rawptr) -> bool"
.B "sendp :: proc(c: ^Chan, p: rawptr)"
.B "recvp :: proc(c: ^Chan) -> rawptr"
.B "sendul :: proc(c: ^Chan, u: u64)"
.B "recvul :: proc(c: ^Chan) -> u64"
.B "alt :: proc(alts: []Alt) -> int"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "qlock :: proc(q: ^QLock)"
.B "qunlock :: proc(q: ^QLock)"
.B "canqlock :: proc(q: ^QLock) -> bool"
.B "rsleep :: proc(r: ^Rendez)"
.B "rwakeup :: proc(r: ^Rendez) -> bool"
.B "rwakeupall :: proc(r: ^Rendez) -> int"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Libthread
gives a program in ring 3 cooperative threads inside one or more
processes, communicating over channels.
A
.I proc
is a Vectra process made by
.I proccreate ,
which shares this one's memory
.RB ( "rfork RFPROC | RFMEM" ,
see
.IR fork (2))
and nothing else it need not.
A
.I thread
is a coroutine inside a proc, made by
.IR threadcreate ,
switched in user space when it blocks on a channel, a lock or
.IR yield ,
and never preempted by another thread of its own proc.
.PP
A program built on
.I libthread
has no
.B main
of its own.
Its
.B _start
sets up
.B context
(see
.IR intro (2))
and calls
.B libthread.main(fn,
.BR arg) ,
which makes the first proc, runs
.I fn
as its only thread, and schedules from then on.
It never returns.
The program ends through
.I threadexits
or
.IR threadexitsall .
.SS Scheduling
The threads of a proc run one at a time, in the order they became ready.
A thread keeps the processor until it calls
.IR yield ,
.IR proccreate ,
an operation on a channel, or a lock or rendezvous call from this
library, and only then does another thread of the same proc run.
Procs are scheduled by the kernel, so threads in different procs may run
at the same time and must synchronize through a
.B QLock
or a channel.
A system call such as
.IR read (2)
blocks the whole proc, and every thread in it.
.PP
.I Threadid
answers the calling thread's id, unique for the life of the program.
.I Yield
lets every other ready thread of the calling proc run once.
.SS Ending
.I Threadexits
ends the calling thread with
.IR status .
When it was the proc's last thread, the proc ends with that status: the
first proc waits for every proc it made before it exits, so the
program's own exit is always the last of its processes, and whoever
.I awaits
the program
.RI ( "see " wait (2))
finds none of them left.
A proc other than the first ends at once when its last thread does.
.PP
.I Threadexitsall
ends the whole program with
.IR status .
It posts a note to every other proc, waits for the ones it made itself,
and exits.
A noted proc with no handler dies at its next kernel boundary, the read
or the rendezvous it is parked in.
Only the first proc may call it.
.SS Channels and alt
A
.B Chan
carries elements of one size, made by
.I chancreate
with capacity
.I nbuf
and freed by
.IR chanfree .
An unbuffered channel,
.I nbuf
zero, is a meeting: a send parks until a receive arrives and the element
passes straight from one to the other.
A buffered channel is a queue: a send parks only when it is full, a
receive only when it is empty.
.PP
.I Send
and
.I recv
pass one element by address.
.I Nbsend
and
.I nbrecv
do the same but answer
.B false
rather than wait when the channel cannot go at once.
.IR Sendp ,
.IR recvp ,
.I sendul
and
.I recvul
are
.I send
and
.I recv
for an element the size of a pointer or a
.BR u64 .
.PP
.I Alt
waits on several channels or sends at once, described by a slice of
.B Alt
arms, and executes and answers the index of the first one that can go.
An arm whose
.I op
is
.B .Noblock
makes
.I alt
answer \-1 instead of waiting when nothing else is ready.
When several arms can go at once, one is chosen at random, so no
channel starves another.
.SS Locks and rendezvous
A
.B QLock
is a lock a thread may hold across a wait: a thread that cannot take it
parks, and its proc runs its other threads meanwhile, unlike the
spinlock of
.IR intro (9),
which a waiter spins on.
.I Qlock
takes it, parking if held;
.I canqlock
takes it only if free, answering whether it did; and
.I qunlock
releases it, handing it directly to the oldest waiter.
.PP
A
.B Rendez
is a condition variable held under a
.BR QLock .
.I Rsleep
releases the lock, parks the calling thread until
.I rwakeup
or
.I rwakeupall
readies it, and takes the lock again before it returns.
The caller re-checks its condition on return, because a wake is a hint
rather than a promise that the condition holds.
.I Rwakeup
readies the oldest sleeper and answers whether there was one.
.I Rwakeupall
readies every sleeper and answers how many there were.
.SH EXAMPLES
A program's
.B _start
hands control to the library and never gets it back:
.IP
.EX
start :: proc "c" (block: ^abi.Args) {
	context = libuser.startup()
	libthread.main(threadmain, nil)
}
.EE
.PP
A proc reports back on a channel:
.IP
.EX
worker :: proc "contextless" (arg: rawptr) {
	c := (^libthread.Chan)(arg)
	libthread.sendul(c, libuser.getpid())
}
libthread.proccreate(worker, c, libthread.STACK_DEFAULT)
pid := libthread.recvul(c)
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libthread
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR wait (2),
.IR notify (2),
.IR ioproc (2),
.IR rendezvous (2)
.SH BUGS
There is no
.I threadnotify
and no note handler for a thread: a note not caught by
.I notify
(see
.IR notify (2))
ends the whole proc.
There is no
.I threadkill
and no
.I threadint
to stop one thread from outside.
A thread has no name
.B /proc
can show, because a thread is not the kernel's.
.I Threadexitsall
may be called only from the first proc; called from any other, it
notes the first proc and returns without waiting for it.
